"Dead on Arrival: Characterizing and Protecting Against Dead-Entry TLB Misses in GPU Microarchitectures (https://arxiv.org/html/2606.00486v2)\nciteturn292view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2606.00486v2\",\"lineno\":50}); Total lines: 374\nL50: GPU workloads with large memory footprints frequently suffer from redundant L2 TLB misses in which a recently evicted translation is immediately re-walked at full page-walk cost. We characterize these dead-entry misses across 24 GPU workloads, finding they account for up to 99% of L2 TLB misses in the most TLB-sensitive applications, yet their performance impact varies widely depending on memory access structure.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://engineering.purdue.edu/tgrogers/publication/rogers-micro-2013/rogers-micro-2013.pdf)\nciteturn292view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 12; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn291view0\",\"pattern\":\"footprint\"}); Total lines: 1484\nL565@P4: Classification the repetition ID is done either by the compiler (pre\u0002dicting that small offsets from the same pointer are in the same line)\nL566@P4: or by hardware (described in Section 5.2.2).\nL567@P4: 5. DIVERGENCE-AWARE WARP\nL568@P4: SCHEDULING (DAWS)\nL569@P4: The goal of DAWS is to keep data in cache that is reused by\nL570@P4: warps executing in loops so that accesses from successive loop it\u0002erations will hit. DAWS does this by first creating a cache-footprint\nL571@P4: prediction for each warp. Then, DAWS only allows load instruc\u0002tions to be issued from warps whose aggregate cache footprints are\nL572@P4: predicted to be captured by the L1D cache.\nL573@P4: Figure 6 illustrates how DAWS works at a high level. A pre\u0002diction of the cache footprint for each warp is created. These pre\u0002dictions are summed to create a total cache footprint. At time T0,\nL574@P4: all warps have no predicted footprint. Warps that enter loops with\nL575@P4: locality are assigned a prediction and consume a portion of the es\u0002timated available cache. When a warp exits the loop its predicted\nL576@P4: footprint is cleared. When the addition of a warp’s prediction to the\nL577@P4: total cache footprint exceeds the effective cache size, that warp is\nL578@P4: prevented from issuing loads. The value of the effective cache size\nL579@P4: is discussed later in Section 5.1. To illustrate DAWS in operation,\nL580@P4: consider what happens at each time-step in Figure 6. Between time\nL581@P4: T0 and T1, warp 0 enters a loop. From a previous code character\u0002ization, DAWS has predicted that this loop has intra-warp locality\nL582@P4: and one divergent load. Sections 5.1 and 5.2 present two variations\nL583@P4: of DAWS that perform this code characterization in different ways.\nL584@P4: Warp 0’s active mask is used to predict that warp 0 will access 32\nL585@P4: cache lines (one for each active lane) in this iteration of the loop.\nL586@P4: The value of the footprint prediction for more complex loops is dis\u0002cussed in detail in Section 5.1.1. Between time T1 and T2, warp 1\nL587@P4: enters the loop with only 16 active threads and receives a smaller\nL588@P4: predicted footprint of 16. Between T2 and T3, warp 2 reaches the\nL589@P4: loop. The addition of Warp 2’s predicted cache footprint to the cur\u0002rent total cache footprint exceeds the effective cache size, therefore\nL590@P4: warp 2 is prevented from issuing any loads. Between T3 and T4,\nL591@P4: 16 of warp 0’s 32 threads have left the loop (causing control flow\nL592@P4: divergence) which frees some predicted cache capacity, allowing\nL593@P4: warp 2 to issue loads again.\nL594@P4: The DAWS warp throttling mechanism is somewhat similar to\nL595@P4: the lost locality scoring system presented in CCWS [33], however\nL596@P4: there are several key differences. In CCWS, scores are assigned\nL597@P4: based on detected lost locality. Warps losing the most locality are\nL598@P4: given more exclusive cache access by preventing warps losing the\nL599@P4: least locality from issuing loads. CCWS is a reactive system that\nL600@P4: has to lose locality before trying to preserve it. DAWS is a proactive\nL601@P4-5: 103Total Cache Footprint (lines)\nL602@P5: Time\nL603@P5: EffCacheSize\nL604@P5: Warp 0's\nL605@P5: CacheFP\nL606@P5: T0 T1 T2\nL607@P5: WZ Warp Cannot\nL608@P5: Issue Loads\nL609@P5: ...\nL610@P5: Legend\nL611@P5: ... ... ...\nL612@P5: T3\nL613@P5: Warp 0\nL614@P5: Enters Loop\nL615@P5: (32 Active)\nL616@P5: W0\nL617@P5: Warp 1\nL618@P5: Enters Loop\nL619@P5: (16 Active)\nL620@P5: W1\nL621@P5: W2\nL622@P5: Warp 2\nL623@P5: Attempts to Enter Loop\nL624@P5: (16 Active)\nL625@P5: No\nL626@P5: Loads\nL627@P5: Warp 0\nL628@P5: 16 threads\nL629@P5: leave loop\nL630@P5: (16 Active) W0\nL631@P5: W1\nL632@P5: W2\nL633@P5: T4\nL634@P5: 32\nL635@P5: 48\nL636@P5: 64\nL637@P5: W1\nL638@P5: W0 W0\nL639@P5: Figure 6: High level view of how DAWS’s cache footprint\nL640@P5: prediction mechanism dynamically throttles the number of\nL641@P5: threads sharing the cache. CacheFP=Cache Footprint\nL642@P5: system that tries to prevent lost locality before it happens. DAWS\nL643@P5: is also proactive in decreasing the level of thread throttling. As\nL644@P5: threads within warps progress through a loop a different number of\nL645@P5: times, the data accessed by their divergent loads is reduced caus\u0002ing DAWS to decrease their predicted cache footprint. DAWS takes\nL646@P5: this control flow divergence into account immediately and scales up\nL647@P5: the number of warps allowed to issue load instructions as appropri\u0002ate. In contrast, CCWS scales back thread throttling by a constant\nL648@P5: factor each cycle, unless more lost locality is detected. In CCWS,\nL649@P5: when warps with the most exclusive cache access stop losing local\u0002ity, their exclusivity is lost and they have to start missing again to\nL650@P5: get it back. DAWS ensures that all warps in loops with intra-warp\nL663@P5: Detected-DAWS. Both versions of DAWS are implemented as an\nL664@P5: extension to the WIA’s baseline warp prioritization logic. The out\u0002put of the scheduler is a Can Issue bit vector that prevents warps\nL665@P5: from issuing. The task of the scheduler is to determine this bit vec\u0002tor. As described in Section 5, this is driven by cache footprint\nL666@P5: predictions.\nL667@P5: To create the cache footprint prediction for each warp, DAWS\nL668@P5: must classify the behaviour of static load instructions in loops. One\nL669@P5: method to predict the behaviour of static load instructions is to do\nL670@P5: a profiling pass of the application. To provide a bound on the po\u0002tential of an online solution, we propose Profiled-DAWS. We clas\u0002sify each static load instruction using the two criteria presented in\nL671@P5: Section 4.2: (1) Is the load converged or diverged? (2) Does the\nL672@P5: load contribute to the footprint for this iteration of the loop (i.e.,\nL673@P5: Warp Issue Arbiter (WIA)\nL674@P5: Dynamic Load Classifier\nL675@P5: (Detected-DAWS Only)\nL676@P5: Prioritize\nL677@P5: Warps\nL678@P5: Can\nL679@P5: Issue\nL680@P5: [1:N]\nL681@P5: Inst.\nL682@P5: To\nL683@P5: Issue\nL684@P5:  (WID/Tag/HasLocality) on load\nL685@P5:  (#access generated) on coalescer result\nL686@P5: Divergence Aware Scheduler\nL687@P5: (Profiled- and Detected-DAWS)\nL688@P5: Static Load Classification Table ...\nL689@P5: IsDiv RepID PCLoad\nL690@P5: Intra-Loop\nL691@P5: Repetition\nL692@P5: Detector\nL693@P5: Cache Footprint Prediction Table\nL694@P5: W1 FootprintPred ...\nL695@P5: # Active\nL696@P5: Lanes\nL697@P5: PCLoopBegin\nL698@P5: Sampling Warp Table ...\nL699@P5: Memory\nL700@P5: Divergence\nL701@P5: Detector ...\nL702@P5: Shader Core\nL703@P5: WIA\nL704@P5: Registers/Execution\nL705@P5: Memory Unit\nL706@P5: L1D\nL707@P5: Cache\nL708@P5: # Active\nL709@P5: Lanes\nL710@P5: Coalescer\nL752@P5: The cache footprint prediction for each warp is stored in the\nL753@P5: cache footprint prediction table ( D ). This table has one entry for\nL754@P5: each warp issue slot on the core. In our baseline this is 32 entries.\nL755@P5: Each entry of the table contains the value of the predicted footprint\nL756@P5: (in cache lines) and the PC identifying the loop (PCLoopBegin).\nL757@P5: The scheduler checks instructions as they are issued, looking for\nL758@P5: loop begin/end points. To identify the loop bounds, we require that\nL765@P6: ISA, which has made it easier to modify the hardware ISA in each\nL766@P6: of the last 3 architecture iterations.\nL767@P6: When the scheduler detects that a warp has issued the first in\u0002struction of a loop, it uses the number of active lanes in the warp\nL768@P6: ( A ) to create the warp’s prediction for this loop iteration. This\nL769@P6: value is written to the warp’s entry in the cache footprint prediction\nL770@P6: table. Section 5.1.1 details how the cache footprint prediction is\nL771@P6: computed. The update logic also writes the PC of the first instruc\u0002tion in the loop to the table (PCLoopBegin). When the warp leaves\nL772@P6: the loop, the prediction table entry for the warp is cleared. To pre\u0002vent deadlock, predicted footprints are also cleared while a warp\nL773@P6: waits at a barrier.\nL774@P6: To determine the aggregate cache footprint, a prefix sum of each\nL775@P6: warp’s cache footprint is performed, starting with the oldest warps.\nL813@P6: summation, each intra-loop repetition group (identified by RepID)\nL814@P6: is only counted once. If there are different divergence characteris\u0002tics within the same repetition ID, then we count it as diverged. In\nL815@P6: our applications, we did not observe a diverged load accessing data\nL816@P6: loaded by a converged load (or vice-versa) in the same loop itera\u0002tion. The result of this summation is written to this warp’s entry in\nL817@P6: the cache footprint prediction table.\nL818@P6: 5.1.2 Predicted Footprint of Warps Outside Loops\nL831@P6: Since warps may skip loops, this may not always be the case, but\nL832@P6: in our applications this approximation is usually true.\nL833@P6: 5.1.3 Dealing with Inner Loops\nL834@P6: DAWS also detects when a warp has entered an inner loop. When\nL835@P6: a warp issuing a new loop begin instruction already has a PCLoopBegin\nL836@P6: value in the cache footprint prediction table that is less than the PC\nL837@P6: of the new instruction, then we assume the warp has entered an in\u0002ner loop. When this happens, the footprint prediction table entry for\nL838@P6: the warp is updated normally, giving the warp the prediction of the\nL839@P6: inner loop. However, when the warp leaves the inner loop, it does\nL840@P6: not clear either the prediction value or the PCLoopBegin. When\nL841@P6: the outer loop begins its next iteration, it detects it is an outer loop\nL842@P6: (because the PC entry in the table is greater than the outer loop’s be\u0002ginning PC) and it recomputes the predicted footprint based on the\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2606.00486] Dead on Arrival: Characterizing and Protecting Against Dead-Entry TLB Misses in GPU Microarchitectures (https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.00486)\nciteturn292view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn291view2\",\"pattern\":\"v2\"}); Total lines: 162\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Hardware Architecture\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 30 May 2026 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 2 Jun 2026 (this version, v2)]\nL24: 